<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Opium War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Opium War was fought in 1839–1842 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, after Chinese efforts to suppress the opium trade. British naval superiority forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nanjing">Treaty of Nanjing</a>, which ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, opened ports to trade and began the era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-opium-war.jpg" alt="First Opium War" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Duncan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Destroying_Chinese_war_junks,_by_E._Duncan_(1843).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> long favoured China, which sustained a trade surplus paid for in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. The British realised they could reduce their deficit by selling opium produced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and sales of Indian opium to China began in 1781 and grew as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> consolidated its control of India. By 1838 the British were selling roughly 1,400 long tons a year. Within the Chinese administration, some officials such as Xu Naiji argued for legalising and taxing opium, while others, later led by Lin Zexu, regarded the trade as a moral evil. The Daoguang Emperor rejected legalisation and in 1839 sent Lin Zexu to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> to halt the trade completely.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In March 1839 Lin forced British dealers to hand over 1,420 tonnes of opium, which he had destroyed in public on Humen Beach on 3 June. Tension rose after British sailors beat to death a villager, Lin Weixi, in July, and Charles Elliot refused to hand the accused over to Chinese authorities. Fighting began after the Qing blockade of foreign ships. The British government, urged by merchants who demanded compensation, sent an expedition that arrived in late June 1840. After the Second Battle of Chuenpi on 7 January 1841 the British occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>.</p><p>Henry Pottinger replaced Elliot as superintendent in August 1841 and moved operations north. British steamships such as the Nemesis could move against winds and tides, and naval guns out-ranged most Qing artillery. In 1842 the fleet advanced up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and captured the emperor's tax barges, sharply reducing his revenue. It took Wusong and Baoshan and occupied the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in June. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanking</a> was signed aboard HMS Cornwallis on 29 August 1842.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty granted an indemnity and extraterritoriality to British subjects, opened five treaty ports and ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> in perpetuity. It was the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a>, and the Treaty of the Bogue recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> as an equal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> concluded similar treaties in 1844. The unrest that followed formed the background of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-opium-war">Second Opium War</a> was fought in 1856 to 1860.</p><p>The war was contested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. William Ewart Gladstone called it a war unjust in its origin, and he feared the judgments of God on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for its iniquity towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Historians still debate whether it was avoidable. Some stress Britain's expanding trade, while others argue that Lord Palmerston's government went to war to uphold national honour. Twentieth-century nationalists in China consider 1839 the start of a century of humiliation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Opium War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Anglo-Sikh Wars brought the Punjab, and the <b>First Opium War</b>, fought to keep the opium trade with Qing China, gained five Chinese ports and Hong Kong under the Treaty of Nanjing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>First Opium War</b> of 1839–1842 exposed the inadequacy of Qing forces against industrialised European militaries: a British fleet with modern artillery and steam-powered warships such as the iron steamer Nemesis overcame Qing coastal and Yangtze defences and forced the Treaty of Nanjing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>For a century afterwards the navy policed the seas, hunted slave ships through the West Africa Squadron and backed the opening of markets, as in the <b>First Opium War</b> with China (1839–1842).</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Chartist papers also denounced imperialism, condemning the <b>First Opium War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/chartism">Chartism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>News of Britain’s victory in the <b>First Opium War</b> was taken as a severe military and cultural disaster.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>First Opium War</b> (1839–1842) had ended in the Treaty of Nanjing, which ceded Hong Kong Island to Britain and opened five ports to foreign trade.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
